<p class="copy">
	The attack, best known from Xi Xutang’s documentary epic <i>Isekai</i>, was first described in <i>Field Notes from Another World: Accounts of the Explorer 18/Dialectic</i>, wherein <i>18</i> recounts how the team was “ambushed by a jagged gloom, nothing but hunger, eyes, spider legs, crocodile teeth, and hate. I looked at the creature and saw nothing, an ink stain in the air, but I could feel it move and I KNEW what it was made of, even if my eyes refused to tell my mind.” The lurid passage describes the survey team’s encounter with a particularly large specimen of an endemic monster species colloquially referred to as “<?php echo sf_lore_link('murk-legs', 'Murk Legs'); ?>” by the local hunting guilds.
</p>
<p class="copy">
	The account continues: “The trees were tight around the port camp, and our sight was limited to, maybe, ten meters at best. Drone overflight couldn’t see through the canopy, but our survey hadn’t given us any indication this planet even supported fauna that large, or fauna at all, which maybe should have given something away. I think it had eaten it all.”
</p>
<p class="copy">
	Suffering two casualties in the first several seconds of the encounter, the team, professionals that they were, maintained their composure and returned fire. When the 10mm sidearms carried by the explorers failed to visibly injure the creature, and with the heavier defensive equipment already locked and stored, team leader Vikram Archest called a quick retreat. Archest led the survivors into the densest nearby forest cover, reasoning that such a sizable predator would struggle to pursue them through the thicket. In macabre truth, Murk Legs are well adapted to navigating dense undergrowth, but they’re also food-motivated carnivores, and this one had already secured a meal.
</p>
<p class="copy">
	Back on <?php echo sf_lore_link('tetra', 'Tetra'); ?>, the black box recordings showed in grainy but gruesome detail the fate that befell the Southtown Surveyors.
</p>
